[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: leaf_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: BGP advertising policy on leaf_router_0_0 (FRR configuration for IPv4 unicast).

Mechanism: The spines no longer have a route to 10.0.0.0/24, so any traffic from 10.0.1.0/24 toward 10.0.0.0/24 has no path beyond the spines. Each PC still pings its local gateway because the directly connected subnet and ARP on the leaf remain fine.

Localization: On leaf_router_0_0, interface eth2 is correctly configured with 10.0.0.1/24 (kernel route present), but BGP on the same device is not originating that prefix. Evidence:
- leaf_router_0_0 interface: eth2 inet 10.0.0.1/24 and kernel route 10.0.0.0/24 dev eth2.
- leaf_router_0_0 FRR config lacks an address-family stanza and network statement for 10.0.0.0/24 (router bgp 65200 has only neighbors; no “address-family ipv4 unicast … network 10.0.0.0/24”).
- Both spines’ routing tables contain B>* 10.0.1.0/24 only; 10.0.0.0/24 is absent.
- leaf_router_0_1 correctly originates 10.0.1.0/24 (address-family ipv4 unicast; network 10.0.1.0/24) and it propagates fabric-wide.

Root cause: A missing BGP network advertisement for 10.0.0.0/24 on leaf_router_0_0.

Corrective fix: On leaf_router_0_0, configure FRR to originate the subnet:
router bgp 65200
 address-family ipv4 unicast
  network 10.0.0.0/24
Then commit/reload FRR. Alternatively, “redistribute connected” in the AFI if that’s the site standard. Verify on spines with “show ip route” that 10.0.0.0/24 appears, and retest pc_0_0 ↔ pc_0_1 ping. === END ===